@packages/compiler/src/report-protocol.sigil import { SigilCompilationReportProtocol }

component SigilCompilationEventProtocol {
  goal {
    Own the closed version-1 compiler event schema and the invariants shared by its
    independently owned writer and reader protocols.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      CompilationEvent is one UTF-8 JSON Lines envelope containing exactly
      protocolVersion, runId, sequence, type, and payload. One optional CR byte 0D
      may precede the LF byte 0A delimiter.

      protocolVersion is exactly 1; runId is one non-empty opaque invocation
      identity; sequence is a JSON integer from 1 through 4294967295 inclusive; type
      is exactly started, stage-started, stage-completed, diagnostic, completed,
      failed, or cancelled.

      ExpectedCompilationRun contains operation, exactly one-shot-compilation or
      session-evaluation, one or more ordered unique non-empty stage identities, and
      one non-empty sessionIdentity exactly when operation is session-evaluation.

      started payload contains exactly operation and sessionIdentity only when
      operation is session-evaluation. stage-started payload contains exactly stage,
      one non-empty stage identity.

      stage-completed payload contains exactly report, one imported
      CompilationStageReport whose id identifies its stage and whose state is
      completed or failed. diagnostic payload contains exactly diagnostic, one
      imported CompilationDiagnostic, and optional componentName, one non-empty
      caller-controlled display identity that is not inferred from semantic subjects.

      completed payload contains exactly report, the authoritative imported
      CompilationReport. failed payload contains exactly code, one non-empty opaque
      stable rejection code other than COMPILER_CANCELLED, and message, one string.
      cancelled payload contains exactly code COMPILER_CANCELLED and message, one
      string. New opaque failed codes do not extend protocol vocabulary.

      Every version-1 envelope and event-owned payload is structurally closed except
      for the opaque failed code. Reject duplicate member names before field
      interpretation. Delegate imported report, stage-report, and diagnostic strict
      decoding, nested duplicate detection, structural validation, equality, and
      diagnostic identity to SigilCompilationReportProtocol ReportWireValidation.

      One stream carries one nonmultiplexed run with invariant runId, consecutive
      delivered sequence beginning at 1, serial nonoverlapping stage intervals, and
      at most one terminal. Delivered stage identities are an order-preserving
      subsequence of ExpectedCompilationRun.

      SigilCompilationEventWriter owns producer establishment, call ordering,
      suppression, and sink delivery. SigilCompilationEventReader independently owns
      byte decoding, cancellation, source lifetime, stream validation, and terminal
      reconciliation. Consumers import only the direction they use plus this schema.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationEventProtocol {
  constraints {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      Unknown types, missing or extra fields, wrong types, invalid type-payload
      pairing, unsupported versions, changed runId, nonconsecutive or out-of-range
      sequence, invalid stage ordering, duplicate diagnostic fingerprints, or an
      event after terminal are protocol-invalid.

      Stage intervals contain stage-started, zero or more diagnostics whose
      originating stage equals the open stage, and stage-completed whose report id
      equals that stage. A terminal may end any valid progress prefix, including one
      with an observably open stage, because later progress can be suppressed.

      Protocol version 1 imposes no normative chunk, frame, envelope, report, or
      accumulated-byte limit. Host capacity failure is a transport availability or
      compatibility outcome, never structural protocol-invalidity.

      Extending an envelope, payload, vocabulary, sequence range, multiplexing rule,
      or ordering rule requires a new protocol version.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      Decision: Use a closed versioned JSON Lines schema with one authoritative
      report in the completed terminal and separate writer and reader owners.

      Scope: Governs shared wire vocabulary and cross-direction invariants; excludes
      transport selection and invoking-operation settlement.

      Trade-offs: Hosts cannot ignore additive fields, multiplex runs, overlap stage
      intervals, or interpret one stream differently by transport.

      Discarded alternatives: Mixed stdout, unframed JSON, permissive extension, and
      one component owning producer and consumer lifecycles were rejected because
      they create ambiguous interoperability or coupled implementation ownership.

      Consequences: Hosts share one exact schema while writer and reader concerns can
      evolve and be imported independently.

      Revisit when: A negotiated later version needs extensions or multiplexing.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      A one-shot started payload omits sessionIdentity; a session started payload
      includes the expected sessionIdentity.

      A changed runId, sequence 4294967296, duplicate envelope or nested report
      member, extra payload field, unknown stage, backward stage, duplicate stage, or
      diagnostic outside its open stage is protocol-invalid.

      A caller may omit diagnostic componentName or supply any non-empty display
      identity without changing diagnostic ownership.
    }
  }
}
